 transmission ranges [Data: Entities (152), Relationships (201, 202, 203)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The Emergence and Advantages of the 6 GHz Band\",\n            \"explanation\": \"A significant development within this community is the introduction and utilization of the 6 GHz band, primarily driven by the Wi-Fi 6E standard. This new spectrum offers substantial benefits over the legacy 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands. The 6 GHz band provides greater bandwidth, enabling higher potential data transfer speeds and improved overall network performance. Crucially, it is designed to operate with significantly less interference because it is a new, less congested spectrum, free from the legacy devices and interference that commonly affect the older bands [Data: Entities (92, 143), Relationships (203)]. This reduction in interference leads to more stable and reliable wireless connections, making the 6 GHz band ideal for bandwidth-intensive applications and for alleviating congestion in densely populated wireless environments [Data: Entities (92), Relationships (145)]. The 'WI-FI 6E' standard is directly associated with the operation within this new 6 GHz frequency range [Data: Entities (107), Relationships (145)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Organizational Structure: Frequency Bands and Channels\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Within the broader concept of Wi-Fi frequency bands, there is an organizational structure based on 'CANAIS_FREQUENCIA_WI-FI' (Wi-Fi frequency channels). These channels represent divisions within the larger frequency bands, allowing for more granular management of wireless traffic and further mitigation of interference. The relationship between channels and bands highlights a hierarchical organization designed to optimize spectrum utilization and network performance [Data: Entities (153), Relationships (204)]. This structured approach is fundamental to how Wi-Fi networks manage communication efficiently 